## Exact lower/upper squeeze

`UST.G3AP.LOWER` independently proves that the selected ordinary top quotient
has dimension at least one. G3AO supplies selected logarithmic regularity on
all 735 Cox orbits; the common big-and-nef Cayley class has top intersection
`14301=60 mod 101`, a unit. Toric residue duality therefore gives a nonzero
selected `R1` top class, and the ordinary partial-Jacobian quotient surjects
onto that logarithmic/saturated quotient.

Consequently a target-specific upper-bound certificate can close G3AP without
claiming a full standard basis. Let `H` be a list emitted by an exact
selected-source standard-basis engine, such as `std` with a Hilbert hint or
`qslimgb` in the same positive Cox order. The engine constructs every element
of `H` from the selected module generators. If all 21 original generators
reduce to zero by `H`, then `H` generates that exact module. The monomial module
generated by `LT(H)` is contained in the true initial module. If its complete
target enumeration leaves only `w^12*t2^4*e6`, the true quotient has dimension
at most one. The residue lower bound supplies dimension at least one, so the
dimension is exactly one and that monomial is the unique target basis.

The same reasoning makes `H` an exact target reduction set for response
products. Division subtracts selected-module elements and leaves only a
multiple of the nonzero target basis. The coefficient is therefore the unique
quotient coordinate even if `H` has not been certified as an unrestricted
standard basis.

The weighted Hilbert, weighted `qslimgb` and plain homogeneous `std`
executions are independent candidate engines. Neither candidate packet is
promotable by process success.
The first one to finish must pass the same selected-base reconstruction,
21-generator replay, complete target enumeration, lower-bound check and
dedicated verifier before canonical adoption.

### Target-preserving L truncation

There is a sharper finite hedge.  In component `j`, the target base coefficient
has fixed `L`-degree `2,5,3,8,6,4`, respectively.  Let `T_L` be the monomial
submodule generated in component `j` by all `t0,t1,t2` monomials of degree one
above that component's target.  It has 165 minimal generators and

```text
(T_L)_(18,2,2) = 0.
```

Therefore `(M+T_L)_(18,2,2)=M_(18,2,2)` exactly.  This is not a numerical
cutoff approximation.  It removes scalar-order detours with excessive
`L`-degree and reduces the counted weighted predecessor cone from 11,483,294
to 7,669,082, removing 3,814,212 candidate monomials.  This count is only a
complexity proxy; the exactness claim comes from the zero target piece and the
independently verified lower/upper squeeze.

A candidate basis of `M+T_L` is accepted only after the verifier replays its
exact construction, checks Cox bihomogeneity, reconstructs the selected base
ideal, and checks all 21 original generators.  Any candidate reducer whose
leader divides a target monomial has total Cox degree at or below the target.
Because `T_L` has no component in such a degree, that reducer belongs to `M`,
even though the unrestricted candidate list need not.  Its effective leading
submodule may therefore provide the exact target upper bound.  This argument
claims neither global equality `M+T_L=M` nor a full standard basis.

