## Why G3AO does not already imply G3AP

Mavlyutov's toric Cayley theorem does apply directly to the ordinary
Jacobian ring of a quasi-smooth ample complete intersection.  In the present
ledger `d=5` and `s=3`; its Hodge degrees include
`3 beta-beta0=(9,1,0)` and `5 beta-beta0=(9,1,2)`.  These are the two
complementary response factors used downstream.  The G3AP target is instead
their product degree

```text
(3 beta-beta0) + (5 beta-beta0) = 8 beta-2 beta0 = (18,2,2).
```

The Hodge identifications and Poincare pairing prove that this product has a
nonzero one-dimensional residue image.  They do not, by themselves, prove
that the ordinary Jacobian target contains no additional irrelevant-boundary
classes.  This is exactly the lower-bound/upper-bound distinction enforced by
the G3AP certificate.

Batyrev and Cox define the toric hypersurface Jacobian ring using the ordinary
partial derivatives and relate selected graded pieces to Hodge theory for a
quasi-smooth ample hypersurface. They also distinguish it from the saturated
logarithmic ideal generated by `z_i*partial_i(Phi)` and explicitly warn that
the precise relation between the two ideals is not understood for a general
toric variety. G3AO proves the selected member is regular on every nonempty
Cox orbit, which controls the logarithmic/saturated residue tier. It does not
by itself determine whether extra irrelevant-boundary classes survive in the
ordinary quotient at `(18,2,2)`.

The G3AP module computation is exactly that missing ordinary-ideal test. It is
therefore not a redundant numerical replay of toric regularity.

Modern terminology makes the distinction especially sharp. A nondegenerate
toric hypersurface whose toric Jacobian quotient has a one-dimensional socle
and perfect graded pairings is called *Macaulay type*. Nondegeneracy and the
Macaulay-type property are stated separately; automatic Macaulay type is
available under additional hypotheses such as Picard number one, which do not
cover this rank-three Cox grading. G3AP is the selected-member certificate of
the required top-line part of that extra property. It must be computed or
proved by an equally strong selected-source theorem.

Villaflor Loyola's toric Macaulay theorem makes the boundary explicit. For an
`m`-dimensional projective simplicial toric variety it gives a one-dimensional
socle for an ideal generated by `m+1` ample Cartier sections with empty common
zero locus. Applied to a nondegenerate toric hypersurface, this controls

```text
J0(Phi) = <z_i partial_i(Phi)>
```

and, after colon by the Cox-coordinate product, selected regular degrees of
`J1(Phi)`. The paper separately observes that the ordinary ideal
`J(Phi)=<partial_i(Phi)>` is not a complete intersection once the Picard rank
is greater than one. Our Cayley ambient has dimension seven and Picard rank
three, while G3AP asks for the ordinary quotient at the `J1` socle degree
`8 beta-2 beta0`. Thus the theorem rigorously supports `G3AP.LOWER`; it does
not supply the missing ordinary-ideal upper bound. The homogeneous-projective
Adolphson--Sperber complex has the same limitation here: it assumes one scalar
projective grading and does not identify this rank-three Cox-graded ordinary
boundary module.

